!YSFlight - riscos1
===================

YSFlight Community Edition, a free flight simulator, for RISC OS.
The game runs in a desktop window, drawn with software OpenGL
(riscos-mesa). Not done yet: the desktop's fonts (the text uses
YSFlight's own bitmap font, Latin-1 only), the online server list and
the air traffic control voice (YSFlight has none on other systems
either).

Needs: RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine with VFP (Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4,
400), SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (PackMan).
PThreadTicker 0.03 is included and loaded by !Run.

Double-click !YSFlight to start. Messages, and a report if it crashes,
go to the file YSFlightLog in the scrap directory
(<Wimp$ScrapDir>.YSFlightLog). Settings are kept in
<Choices$Write>.YSFlight.

Sound: engines, guns, alarms and the other effects play through
UnixLib's /dev/dsp. Best with John Duffell's SharedSoundBuffer modules
(SharedSound 1.07, StreamManager 0.03 and SharedSoundBuffer 0.07: merge
the !System from ssb.zip, on Andrew Sellors' RDPClient page,
https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html); !Run loads them,
and the sound is then mixed with other programs'. Without them it uses
DigitalRenderer, if it's loaded. The Sound tick box (Option menu)
switches it off.

Keys and mouse: as YSFlight on other systems. Select = left button,
Menu = middle, Adjust = right. Click in the window to give it the
keyboard.
In full screen the pointer stops just above the bottom of the screen,
so the icon bar doesn't pop up over the game (*Set
YSFlight$PointerMargin 0 to turn that off).

Joysticks: USB joysticks and gamepads work through Richard Walker's
USBJoystick module (0.23 or later gives every axis, the hat and all
buttons, for up to 8 devices; load it before starting YSFlight). Other
joystick modules that provide Joystick_Read give one stick with X, Y and
buttons. Choose which axis and button does what in Option >
Joystick/Key Assignments, and calibrate in Option > Calibrate
Joystick; the calibration is kept in <Choices$Write>.YSFlight.joycalib.

Speed: software OpenGL draws every pixel with the processor, so a
smaller window, or a smaller picture stretched to the window, is
faster. See the YSFlight$Size and YSFlight$Render lines in !Run.
The two costliest options are off in a new RISC OS configuration:
"Draw Burning Smoke by Particles" (Option > Config, Graphics tab; it
also draws the clouds as particles) and "Use Ground Texture" (OpenGL
tab). A configuration saved by an earlier test build keeps its own
values, so untick them there.

ServerTests (in !YSFlight) runs the console server tests in a task
window; they write to !YSFlight.Logs.

This is an unofficial port, not made or supported by the YSCE
developers. AI (Anthropic's Claude) has been used as a coding assistant.

